  "hideUi": true,
  "description": "A/B for vector_light_parallel_coverage -- splitting ONE emitter's coverage grid into row bands across threads -- on the frame the whole-emitter fan-out cannot help with. Same plate and door mechanics as vector_light_draw_door.json: a sealed room, one door, two flanking TorchLamps, the clock RUNNING (a door's OpenPct comes off Building_Door.Tick, so a TickLapse jump never swings it), swung six times per arm. Two emitters is deliberately BELOW VectorLightField's four-emitter fan-out threshold, so the batch never spreads across threads and every bake arrives with rowThreads true -- which is exactly the frame the row split exists for and the one the emitter split leaves untouched. Four arms, off/on/off/on, so the second pair says whether the first pair's difference survives the machine: this box has moved an unchanged binary by a factor of two between runs, and a single pair cannot tell that from a result. vector_light_bake_wall_ms is the metric that scores it -- the Circinus arms report time exclusive of their armed children and a thread split MOVES time rather than removing it, so only a clock on the calling thread sees the saving. The two pass counters say the path was taken at all, read as a pair because a zero on the fan-out count alone cannot separate a refused threshold from a scene of emitters too small to qualify. The gather and upload clocks are the two thirds of the frame this change does not touch and stand as controls on the run. Captures are taken at each arm's end for the record and CANNOT judge inertness here, which is the cost of a scenario whose clock has to run: the scene animates between arms, and the same-build control (off_a against off_b) measures a LARGER median CIELAB difference than the A/B pair does, on ~99% of pixels in both. What holds the grid identical is offline instead -- VectorLightCoverageBandTests compares every banding of a grid against VectorLightCoverageOracle, an independent implementation -- plus the vector_light_suite.txt gate, whose gap-and-door L* table is a measurement of the same coverage grid this splits.",
